  "seven_q": {
    "Q0_posture": "Critical and self-reflective; the document is an honest assessment that acknowledges its own weaknesses and potential biases.",
    "Q1_identity": "An isomorphism record analyzing the consilience between biblical cosmological language and modern physics, with a strong emphasis on falsification and honest assessment.",
    "Q2_domain": "Intersection of philosophy of science, Christian theology, biblical studies, Hebrew linguistics, and cosmology.",
    "Q3_claim": "The consilience between biblical cosmological language (stretched heavens, Logos) and modern physics (cosmic expansion, information theory) is weak; the stretched heavens correspondence is likely a false positive due to translation ambiguity and selection bias; the Logos correspondence is partial; GCP/PEAR data is too contested to anchor the claim.",
    "Q4_support": "Hebrew linguistic analysis of natah, ANE comparative cosmology, falsification tests (5 specific tests), honest assessment of cherry-picking, and acknowledgment of counterexamples (firmament, pillars, flat earth).",
    "Q5_dependencies": "Assumes validity of Whewell's consilience framework; assumes GCP/PEAR data is valid (contested); assumes biblical texts can contain genuine cosmological insight; requires Hebrew lexicography and ANE context.",
    "Q6_consequences": "Weakens the case for biblical cosmological predictive power; suggests the isomorphism is analogical rather than structural; requires reframing from surface-level correspondence to deeper theological meaning; raises questions about retrospective prediction as genuine prediction.",
    "Q7_kill": "If Hebrew linguistic analysis definitively shows natah means only tent-pitching (not expansion), if ANE parallels are exact, if GCP/PEAR data collapses, or if the Logos-information correspondence is shown to be a false cognate."
  },
